<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Aligning generative recommender systems to user preferences via post-training is critical for closing the gap between next-item prediction and actual recommendation quality. Existing post-training methods are ill-suited for production-scale systems: RLHF methods reward hack due to noisy user feedback and unreliable reward models, offline RL alternatives require propensity scores that are unavailable, and online interaction is infeasible. We identify exponential reward-weighted SFT with weights <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=\exp(r/\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=\exp(r/\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> as uniquely suited to this setting, and provide the theoretical and empirical foundations that explain why. By optimizing directly on observed rewards without querying a learned reward model, the method is immune to reward hacking, requires no propensity scores, and is fully offline. We prove the first policy improvement guarantees for this setting under noisy rewards, showing that the gap scales only logarithmically with catalog size and remains informative even for large item catalogs. Crucially, we show that temperature <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> explicitly and quantifiably controls the robustness-improvement tradeoff, providing practitioners with a single interpretable regularization hyperparameter with theoretical grounding. Experiments on three open-source and one proprietary dataset against four baselines confirm that exponential reward weighting is simple, scalable, and consistently outperforms RLHF-based alternatives.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>Generative Recommenders, Post-training
</div><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_affiliationnotice"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">affiliationnotice: </span>Equal contributionWork done while KC was an intern at Netflix Research.</span></span></span>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The success of transformer architectures has led to the rise of generative recommenders. This paradigm treats recommendations as a sequential generation problem where recommendations are generated sequentially based on the user’s interaction history <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deldjoo et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, analogous to how Large Language Models (LLMs) predict the next token given a sequence of text <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rajput et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhai et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kang and McAuley, 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deng et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Vanilla generative recommender architectures such as SASRec <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kang and McAuley, 2018</a>)</cite>, HSTU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhai et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and OneRec <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deng et al., 2025</a>)</cite> model user behavior through large-scale behavior cloning. However, purely imitating users through behavior cloning leads to indiscriminate mimicking, where the model learns high-value engagements (like items they enjoyed) and low-value engagements (like accidental clicks or click-bait interactions) with equal importance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Motivated by recent advances in Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stiennon et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, this paper explores post-training alignment of generative recommenders with rich user feedback. The core insight from RLHF in language models is that post-training optimization with preference data can significantly improve model quality beyond what behavior cloning alone achieves. This paradigm is particularly promising for recommendation systems, where massive organic user feedback is naturally available at scale—including implicit signals such as watch time, completion rates, and re-engagement patterns, as well as explicit signals such as ratings and reviews. By leveraging this abundant feedback to move beyond pure behavior cloning, we can potentially train recommenders that distinguish high-value from low-value engagements and explore beyond the confines of observed user trajectories. However, adapting RLHF techniques from language models to generative recommenders introduces unique challenges due to the following factors.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reward Model Unreliability.</span> Item representations in generative recommenders are learned purely from behavioral data with no semantic grounding. Since users interact with only a small fraction of the catalog <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dulac-Arnold et al., 2015</a>)</cite>, a reward model must extrapolate over the vast majority of items from sparse supervision, a generalization problem that grows severe at scale. During policy optimization, the policy exploits these extrapolation errors, systematically selecting items for which the reward model is overoptimistic rather than those that truly maximize user satisfaction.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Offline Learning Constraints.</span> In industrial settings the learning dataset is pre-collected and static, making interactive feedback loops infeasible. RLHF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stiennon et al., 2020</a>)</cite> requires a reward model as a simulator. DPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al., 2023</a>)</cite> avoids this but requires binary preference pairs, whereas recommendation feedback is scalar. In practice, overlapping interactions covering the full catalog are too sparse to construct such pairs without again relying on a learned reward model.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lack of Logging Policy.</span> The offline dataset exhibits selection bias, as rewards are only observed for actions taken by the logging policy. While Inverse Propensity Scoring (IPS) could theoretically correct this, the logging policy is often too complex <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Covington et al., 2016</a>)</cite> and inaccessible to estimate <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang and Vlassis, 2022</a>)</cite>, and IPS weights suffer from extreme variance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Swaminathan and Joachims, 2015</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dudík et al., 2011</a>)</cite>, particularly when the logging policy is nearly deterministic.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To overcome these challenges, we propose Exponential Reward-Weighted SFT (Exp-RSFT), which weights training examples by <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp(r/\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp(r/\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> using only observed rewards. By never querying a learned reward model, the method sidesteps reward model unreliability at its root. It cannot reward hack, requires no propensity scores, and is fully offline. While exponential reward weighting has appeared in prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peters and Schaal, 2007</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang and Vlassis, 2022</a>)</cite>, its suitability for large-scale generative recommenders has not been theoretically justified nor empirically validated at production scale. We make the following contributions:</p>
<ul id="S1.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reward models fail catastrophically in the generative recommender setting.</span> Reward models must extrapolate over a vast item catalog from sparse observations, making them unreliable surrogates for true user preferences. We show empirically that the learned reward model fails to outperform naive item-mean prediction, yet PPO and DPO over-optimize for it, collapsing catastrophically on true recommendation metrics.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Temperature <math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> provably controls the robustness-improvement tradeoff under noisy rewards.</span> Observed rewards are inherently noisy even without a learned reward model. We prove policy improvement guarantees under noisy rewards with only logarithmic dependence on catalog size, and a closed-form characterization of the robustness-improvement tradeoff controlled by <math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. The <math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> sweep produces a consistent inverted-U performance curve across all datasets, empirically validating the theory.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exp-RSFT consistently outperforms all baselines across four datasets.</span> Exp-RSFT consistently outperforms all baselines across three public benchmarks and a large-scale proprietary dataset. Gains over PPO and DPO stem from avoiding the reward model entirely; gains over behavior cloning and linear reward-weighted SFT isolate <math id="S1.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> as the key ingredient, which provides explicit control over re-ranking aggressiveness and robustness to noise in a single interpretable parameter.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Literature</h2>

<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<p id="S2.F1.3" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center ltx_align_center"><span id="S2.F1.3.1" class="ltx_text"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.10279v1/tradeoff.svg" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/306;" width="476" height="306"></object></span></p>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S2.F1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
Comparison of policy learning methods along two axes: best achievable policy performance and dependency on a good reward model.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Reinforcement Learning (RL) based post-training methods for generative models span a spectrum of reward model dependency and achievable performance (<a href="#S2.F1" title="In 2 Related Literature ‣ Robust Post-Training for Generative Recommenders: Why Exponential Reward-Weighted SFT Outperforms RLHF" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) that typically includes Online RL, Offline RL and Behavior Cloning. Our method sits the space between Offline RL and Behavior Cloning, where it leverages user preferences but in a limited manner. We review the related approaches and position our method within this landscape.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Standard post-training methods for generative models include RLHF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stiennon et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, DPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and GRPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. RLHF optimizes a policy via PPO against a learned reward model. GRPO follows the same paradigm but replaces the learned value function baseline with a group average reward. Both could in principle be applied to generative recommender post-training, but the learned reward model is the central bottleneck: in recommendation settings, reward models generalize poorly across the full item catalog. Any method that queries a learned reward model at training time inherits this generalization failure, regardless of the policy optimization strategy used on top. DPO sidesteps reward model optimization but requires binary preference pairs. Recommendation feedback such as ratings, watch time, and reviews are scalar in nature, and obtaining pairwise comparisons for unobserved items still requires a learned reward model, reintroducing the generalization problem. Offline RL methods like CQL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kumar et al., 2020</a>)</cite> offer principled alternatives by learning conservative Q-functions, but these must also generalize across the massive item space and face the same challenges. Doubly robust methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dudík et al., 2011</a>)</cite> and counterfactual risk minimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Swaminathan and Joachims, 2015</a>)</cite> require inverse propensity scores, which are intractable to compute for complex production pipelines.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Our method is closely related to weighted behavior cloning algorithms that use monotonic functions of rewards as weights <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peters and Schaal, 2007</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang and Vlassis, 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mukherjee et al., </a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kostrikov et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. Like our approach, these methods operate on offline data and do not require propensity scores. Most notably, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peters and Schaal, 2007</a>)</cite> introduces the same softmax reward weighting scheme, though their analysis focuses on training stability. We focus on a different perspective: statistical guarantees under noisy rewards and the algorithm’s suitability for post-training generative recommenders. MARWIL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2018</a>)</cite>, IQL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kostrikov et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, CRR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, and LPI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang and Vlassis, 2022</a>)</cite> weight log probabilities by <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp(\text{A}/\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext>A</mtext><mo>/</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp(\text{A}/\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is the advantage function. In the bandit setting, weighting by <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp(r/\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp(r/\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> optimizes the same objective, as the state-dependent value function in the advantage cancels within the softmax <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang and Vlassis, 2022</a>)</cite>. This equivalence makes explicit advantage estimation unnecessary in our case, a significant departure from methods like IQL that require learning separate <math id="S2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> functions via expectile regression.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mukherjee et al. ()</a></cite> considers a simpler variant that weights linearly by rewards. This similarly avoids reward model dependence, but the resulting policy is proportional to the product of reward and logging frequency, conflating item quality with popularity. Additionally, linear reward weighting is not robust to noisy observed rewards. In contrast, weighting by <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp(r/\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp(r/\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> introduces <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> as an intrinsic regularizer that both controls sensitivity to reward noise and decouples item quality from logging frequency: as <math id="S2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> decreases, the policy concentrates on high-reward items regardless of their original popularity. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mukherjee et al. ()</a></cite> also relies on standardization to control variance; in our method, standardization is unnecessary as additive shifts cancel in the softmax and tuning <math id="S2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> achieves the same effect as scaling. Methods that weight linearly with rewards or advantages face an additional challenge: negative weights lead to negative log likelihoods. In recommendation settings this is particularly harmful, as decreasing probability on observed items reallocates mass to unobserved items, which typically have lower true preference due to selection bias <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Steck, 2010</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schnabel et al., 2016</a>)</cite>, thereby leading to worse performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Reward-weighted methods have also gained traction for LLM post-training, notably RLOO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ahmadian et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and ALoL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Baheti et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. However, ALoL requires propensity scores and a learned value function for computing advantages. RLOO generates multiple outputs and scores each with a reward model, weighting by the reward relative to the average across generations. Since we cannot generate new recommendations and observe their rewards, RLOO requires reward model predictions, reintroducing generalization issues. It also inherits the negative log-likelihood problem since rewards relative to the average can be negative.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Background</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Problem Setting</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We model recommendation as a contextual bandit with environment <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}=(\mathcal{S},\mathcal{A},r,d_{0})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}=(\mathcal{S},\mathcal{A},r,d_{0}),</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> is the context space, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> the action space, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r:\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{A}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r:\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{A}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> the reward function, and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> the context distribution. In the generative recommender setting, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> represents a user’s interaction history and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> the next item to recommend. We assume access to an offline dataset <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}=\{(s_{i},a_{i},r_{i})\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=\{(s_{i},a_{i},r_{i})\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> collected under an unknown logging policy or mixture of policies. Since our objective is to maximize immediate reward, we model each recommendation independently as a contextual bandit. For a stochastic policy <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(a\mid s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(a\mid s)</annotation></semantics></math>, the value and advantage functions are <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="V^{\pi}(s)=\mathbb{E}_{a\sim\pi(\cdot\mid s)}[r(s,a)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\pi}(s)=\mathbb{E}_{a\sim\pi(\cdot\mid s)}[r(s,a)]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\pi}(s,a)=r(s,a)-V^{\pi}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\pi}(s,a)=r(s,a)-V^{\pi}(s)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Exponential Reward-Weighted Policy Optimization</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our policy optimization objective is the same as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nair et al. (2020)</a></cite>. The original arguments in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nair et al. (2020)</a></cite> can be extended to obtain our algorithm, we re-state it here for completeness.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">At each iteration <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, we seek a new policy <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math> that maximizes expected advantage under the behavior policy’s state visitation distribution, while remaining close to the data-generating distribution <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\pi_{k+1}=\arg\max_{\pi}\mathbb{E}_{s\sim d^{\pi_{\beta}},a\sim\pi(\cdot|s)}\left[A^{\pi_{k}}(s,a)\right]\quad" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>arg</mi><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>π</mi></munder><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>d</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>β</mi></msub></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pi_{k+1}=\arg\max_{\pi}\mathbb{E}_{s\sim d^{\pi_{\beta}},a\sim\pi(\cdot|s)}\left[A^{\pi_{k}}(s,a)\right]\quad</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\text{s.t.}\quad\mathbb{E}_{s\sim d^{\pi_{\beta}}}\left[D_{\mathrm{KL}}\left(\pi(\cdot|s)\|\pi_{\beta}(\cdot|s)\right)\right]\leq\epsilon," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>s.t.</mtext><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>β</mi></msub></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{s.t.}\quad\mathbb{E}_{s\sim d^{\pi_{\beta}}}\left[D_{\mathrm{KL}}\left(\pi(\cdot|s)\|\pi_{\beta}(\cdot|s)\right)\right]\leq\epsilon,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> must be a valid policy satisfying <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{a}\pi(a|s)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>a</mi></msub><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{a}\pi(a|s)=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(a|s)\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(a|s)\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s,a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s,a</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The Lagrangian of this constrained optimization problem is:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}(\pi,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}(\pi,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{s\sim d^{\pi_{\beta}}}\left[\mathbb{E}_{a\sim\pi(\cdot|s)}\left[A^{\pi_{k}}(s,a)\right]\right." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>β</mi></msub></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{s\sim d^{\pi_{\beta}}}\left[\mathbb{E}_{a\sim\pi(\cdot|s)}\left[A^{\pi_{k}}(s,a)\right]\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\quad\left.-\lambda D_{\mathrm{KL}}\left(\pi(\cdot|s)\|\pi_{\beta}(\cdot|s)\right)\right]+\lambda\epsilon." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad\left.-\lambda D_{\mathrm{KL}}\left(\pi(\cdot|s)\|\pi_{\beta}(\cdot|s)\right)\right]+\lambda\epsilon.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Taking the functional derivative with respect to <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> and setting it to zero yields the closed-form solution:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}(a|s)=\frac{1}{Z(s)}\pi_{\beta}(a|s)\exp\left(\frac{1}{\lambda}A^{\pi_{k}}(s,a)\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>λ</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}(a|s)=\frac{1}{Z(s)}\pi_{\beta}(a|s)\exp\left(\frac{1}{\lambda}A^{\pi_{k}}(s,a)\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z(s)=\sum_{a^{\prime}}\pi_{\beta}(a^{\prime}|s)\exp\left(\frac{1}{\lambda}A^{\pi_{k}}(s,a^{\prime})\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>λ</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z(s)=\sum_{a^{\prime}}\pi_{\beta}(a^{\prime}|s)\exp\left(\frac{1}{\lambda}A^{\pi_{k}}(s,a^{\prime})\right)</annotation></semantics></math> is the state-dependent partition function. This is the algoritm presented in the paper <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nair et al. (2020)</a></cite>. But we can simplify this further for our setting.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In a contextual bandit formulation, the advantage decomposes as <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\pi}(s,a)=r(s,a)-V^{\pi}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\pi}(s,a)=r(s,a)-V^{\pi}(s)</annotation></semantics></math>, where the baseline <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\pi}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\pi}(s)</annotation></semantics></math> depends only on the state. Substituting into Equation (<a href="#S3.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ 3.2 Exponential Reward-Weighted Policy Optimization ‣ 3 Background ‣ Robust Post-Training for Generative Recommenders: Why Exponential Reward-Weighted SFT Outperforms RLHF" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>):</p>
<table id="S3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\pi^{*}(a|s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pi^{*}(a|s)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{Z(s)}\pi_{\beta}(a|s)\exp\left(\frac{r(s,a)-V^{\pi_{k}}(s)}{\lambda}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mi>λ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{Z(s)}\pi_{\beta}(a|s)\exp\left(\frac{r(s,a)-V^{\pi_{k}}(s)}{\lambda}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{Z(s)}\pi_{\beta}(a|s)\exp\left(\frac{r(s,a)}{\lambda}\right)\exp\left(\frac{-V^{\pi_{k}}(s)}{\lambda}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>λ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mi>λ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{Z(s)}\pi_{\beta}(a|s)\exp\left(\frac{r(s,a)}{\lambda}\right)\exp\left(\frac{-V^{\pi_{k}}(s)}{\lambda}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp\left(\frac{-V^{\pi_{k}}(s)}{\lambda}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mi>λ</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp\left(\frac{-V^{\pi_{k}}(s)}{\lambda}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> is constant with respect to actions, it cancels out along with the partition function <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z(s)</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}(a|s)=\frac{\pi_{\beta}(a|s)\exp\left(\frac{r(s,a)}{\lambda}\right)}{\sum_{a^{\prime}}\pi_{\beta}(a^{\prime}|s)\exp\left(\frac{r(s,a^{\prime})}{\lambda}\right)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>λ</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>λ</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}(a|s)=\frac{\pi_{\beta}(a|s)\exp\left(\frac{r(s,a)}{\lambda}\right)}{\sum_{a^{\prime}}\pi_{\beta}(a^{\prime}|s)\exp\left(\frac{r(s,a^{\prime})}{\lambda}\right)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_observation">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Observation 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> </span>(Baseline and Scale Invariance)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the contextual bandit setting, the optimal policy in Equation (<a href="#S3.E7" title="Equation 7 ‣ 3.2 Exponential Reward-Weighted Policy Optimization ‣ 3 Background ‣ Robust Post-Training for Generative Recommenders: Why Exponential Reward-Weighted SFT Outperforms RLHF" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) exhibits two invariances:</p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baseline invariance:</span> For any state-dependent function <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b(s)</annotation></semantics></math>, adding it to the rewards leaves the optimal policy unchanged:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\pi^{*}(a|s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pi^{*}(a|s)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\propto\pi_{\beta}(a|s)\exp\left(\frac{r(s,a)+b(s)}{\lambda}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∝</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mi>λ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\propto\pi_{\beta}(a|s)\exp\left(\frac{r(s,a)+b(s)}{\lambda}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\propto\pi_{\beta}(a|s)\exp\left(\frac{r(s,a)}{\lambda}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∝</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>λ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\propto\pi_{\beta}(a|s)\exp\left(\frac{r(s,a)}{\lambda}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp(b(s)/\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp(b(s)/\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> is constant with respect to <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>, it cancels in the normalization. Consequently, the policy depends only on rewards <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(s,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(s,a)</annotation></semantics></math>, not advantages <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\pi}(s,a)=r(s,a)-V^{\pi}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\pi}(s,a)=r(s,a)-V^{\pi}(s)</annotation></semantics></math>. This eliminates the need to estimate a value function, removing a source of approximation error.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scale invariance:</span> For any constant <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, scaling rewards by <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to rescaling temperature: <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp(c\cdot r/\lambda)=\exp(r/(\lambda/c))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp(c\cdot r/\lambda)=\exp(r/(\lambda/c))</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> subsumes reward scaling, and we may use unnormalized rewards without loss of generality.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Together, these properties yield a simple algorithm with a single interpretable hyperparameter <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> that controls regularization strength. As in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nair et al. (2020)</a></cite>, in order to obtain a parametric policy <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> (where <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the policy parameters), we project the non-parametric solution onto the policy class by minimizing the KL divergence:</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\theta_{k+1}=\arg\min_{\theta}\mathbb{E}_{s\sim d^{\pi_{\beta}}}\left[D_{\mathrm{KL}}\left(\pi^{*}(\cdot|s)\|\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|s)\right)\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>arg</mi><munder><mi>min</mi><mi>θ</mi></munder><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>β</mi></msub></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{k+1}=\arg\min_{\theta}\mathbb{E}_{s\sim d^{\pi_{\beta}}}\left[D_{\mathrm{KL}}\left(\pi^{*}(\cdot|s)\|\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|s)\right)\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Expanding the KL divergence and dropping terms independent of <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\theta_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\theta_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\arg\max_{\theta}\mathbb{E}_{s\sim d^{\pi_{\beta}}}\left[\mathbb{E}_{a\sim\pi^{*}(\cdot|s)}\left[\log\pi_{\theta}(a|s)\right]\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>arg</mi><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>θ</mi></munder><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>β</mi></msub></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\arg\max_{\theta}\mathbb{E}_{s\sim d^{\pi_{\beta}}}\left[\mathbb{E}_{a\sim\pi^{*}(\cdot|s)}\left[\log\pi_{\theta}(a|s)\right]\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\arg\max_{\theta}\mathbb{E}_{s\sim d^{\pi_{\beta}},a\sim\pi_{\beta}(\cdot|s)}\left[\frac{\pi^{*}(a|s)}{\pi_{\beta}(a|s)}\log\pi_{\theta}(a|s)\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>arg</mi><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>θ</mi></munder><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>d</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>β</mi></msub></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>β</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\arg\max_{\theta}\mathbb{E}_{s\sim d^{\pi_{\beta}},a\sim\pi_{\beta}(\cdot|s)}\left[\frac{\pi^{*}(a|s)}{\pi_{\beta}(a|s)}\log\pi_{\theta}(a|s)\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\arg\max_{\theta}\mathbb{E}_{(s,a)\sim\mathcal{D}}\left[\exp\left(\frac{r(s,a)}{\lambda}\right)\log\pi_{\theta}(a|s)\right]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>θ</mi></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>λ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\arg\max_{\theta}\mathbb{E}_{(s,a)\sim\mathcal{D}}\left[\exp\left(\frac{r(s,a)}{\lambda}\right)\log\pi_{\theta}(a|s)\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p7.2" class="ltx_p">where we used importance sampling to convert the expectation over <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> to an expectation over <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>, and dropped the partition function <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z(s)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Equation (<a href="#S3.E11" title="Equation 11 ‣ 3.2 Exponential Reward-Weighted Policy Optimization ‣ 3 Background ‣ Robust Post-Training for Generative Recommenders: Why Exponential Reward-Weighted SFT Outperforms RLHF" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>) corresponds to a weighted maximum likelihood objective where actions from the offline dataset are weighted by exponentiated scaled rewards, yielding Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3.2 Exponential Reward-Weighted Policy Optimization ‣ 3 Background ‣ Robust Post-Training for Generative Recommenders: Why Exponential Reward-Weighted SFT Outperforms RLHF" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. The algorithm is simple to implement via standard SFT APIs and requires neither a reward model, importance sampling, nor knowledge of the logging policy <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>. The only hyperparameter is <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, which also implicitly controls regularization strength. In the next section, we analyze the robustness of this method to noise in <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, providing both a high-probability policy improvement guarantee and an instance-dependent top-<math id="S3.SS2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> recoverability guarantee under noisy rewards.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Exponential Reward Weighted SFT</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> Offline dataset <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}=\{(s_{i},a_{i},r_{i})\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=\{(s_{i},a_{i},r_{i})\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, initial policy <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, temperature <math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, number of epochs <math id="alg1.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1,\ldots,T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1,\ldots,T</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> each mini-batch <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\subset\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\subset\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
               Compute loss:

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
               <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\theta)\leftarrow-\frac{1}{|B|}\displaystyle\sum_{(s,a,r)\in B}\exp\left(\frac{r}{\lambda}\right)\cdot\log\pi_{\theta}(a\mid s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>r</mi><mi>λ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\theta)\leftarrow-\frac{1}{|B|}\displaystyle\sum_{(s,a,r)\in B}\exp\left(\frac{r}{\lambda}\right)\cdot\log\pi_{\theta}(a\mid s)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
               Update: <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\leftarrow\theta-\eta\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{L}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\leftarrow\theta-\eta\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{L}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output:</span> Fine-tuned policy <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Theoretical Analysis</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We begin our analysis of Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3.2 Exponential Reward-Weighted Policy Optimization ‣ 3 Background ‣ Robust Post-Training for Generative Recommenders: Why Exponential Reward-Weighted SFT Outperforms RLHF" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> with a warm-up exercise that assumes that the offline dataset contains the true ground truth rewards <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. The monotonic policy improvement proof from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2018</a>)</cite> can be extended for this case to give the following policy improvement result (full proof in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 Proposition Proof ‣ Appendix A Proofs ‣ Robust Post-Training for Generative Recommenders: Why Exponential Reward-Weighted SFT Outperforms RLHF" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>):</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Monotonic Policy Improvement)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}_{\lambda}(a|s)\propto\pi_{\beta}(a|s)\exp\left(\frac{r^{*}(s,a)}{\lambda}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>λ</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}_{\lambda}(a|s)\propto\pi_{\beta}(a|s)\exp\left(\frac{r^{*}(s,a)}{\lambda}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then in the contextual bandit setting:</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V^{\pi^{*}_{\lambda}}(s)\geq V^{\pi_{\beta}}(s)\quad\text{for all }s\in\mathcal{S}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>β</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">for all </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V^{\pi^{*}_{\lambda}}(s)\geq V^{\pi_{\beta}}(s)\quad\text{for all }s\in\mathcal{S}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This simple proof shows that by performing SFT with exponentially-weighted rewards scaled by a temperature, we obtain a policy that is monotonically better than the logging policy. However, in practice, user feedback signals are inherently stochastic: a user’s rating for the same item may vary across sessions due to mood, context, or attention; click behavior is noisy and influenced by position bias; and implicit signals like dwell time fluctuate based on external factors unrelated to true preference. Next, we aim to understand policy improvement from this noisy reward perspective. We being by making the following simplifying assumptions on the noise:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Sub-Gaussian Reward Noise)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Observed rewards satisfy <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{r}(s,a)=r^{*}(s,a)+\xi(s,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{r}(s,a)=r^{*}(s,a)+\xi(s,a)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the true expected reward for state-action pair <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s,a)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\xi(s,a)\}_{a\in\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\xi(s,a)\}_{a\in\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> are independent, zero-mean, <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-sub-Gaussian random variables.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The sub-gaussian condition is standard and encompasses most reward distributions in recommender systems: bounded signals such as discrete ratings or binary clicks are sub-Gaussian by Hoeffding’s lemma, as are continuous rewards with Gaussian noise. The zero-mean and independence conditions are standard in stochastic bandit analysis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Auer et al., 2002</a>)</cite>. Given this noise structure, we derive the following guarantee (see Appendix <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 Theorem Proof ‣ Appendix A Proofs ‣ Robust Post-Training for Generative Recommenders: Why Exponential Reward-Weighted SFT Outperforms RLHF" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a> for the full proof):</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Policy Improvement Under Noisy Rewards)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> be the behavior policy and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> be the policy obtained by exponential reward-weighted SFT with temperature <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> using noisy rewards <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{r}=r^{*}+\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{r}=r^{*}+\xi</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-sub-Gaussian (Assumption <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Assumption 4.2 (Sub-Gaussian Reward Noise). ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Robust Post-Training for Generative Recommenders: Why Exponential Reward-Weighted SFT Outperforms RLHF" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>). With probability at least <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\delta</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\pi_{\lambda}}(s)\geq V^{\pi_{\beta}}(s)-2\sigma\sqrt{2\log(2|\mathcal{A}|/\delta)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>β</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\pi_{\lambda}}(s)\geq V^{\pi_{\beta}}(s)-2\sigma\sqrt{2\log(2|\mathcal{A}|/\delta)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{A}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{A}|</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of actions.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The gap scales as <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(\sigma\sqrt{\log|\mathcal{A}|})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(\sigma\sqrt{\log|\mathcal{A}|})</annotation></semantics></math>, so the logarithmic dependence on catalog size means the bound remains informative even for large item catalogs. In the noiseless limit (<math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>), the bound recovers the exact monotonic improvement guarantee of Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Proposition 4.1 (Monotonic Policy Improvement). ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Robust Post-Training for Generative Recommenders: Why Exponential Reward-Weighted SFT Outperforms RLHF" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>. Note that the bound holds pointwise for each state <math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>; a uniform guarantee over all states would require an additional union bound over <math id="S4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{S}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{S}|</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding an <math id="S4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(\sigma\sqrt{\log(|\mathcal{S}||\mathcal{A}|)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(\sigma\sqrt{\log(|\mathcal{S}||\mathcal{A}|)})</annotation></semantics></math> dependence. Note that this bound is independent of the regularizer/temperature <math id="S4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, if the observed rewards are bounded, we are able to work out a temperature-dependent bound as follows (full proof in <a href="#A1.SS3" title="A.3 Theorem Proof ‣ Appendix A Proofs ‣ Robust Post-Training for Generative Recommenders: Why Exponential Reward-Weighted SFT Outperforms RLHF" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a>):</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.4</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Temperature-Dependent Bound)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under the conditions of Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 4.3 (Policy Improvement Under Noisy Rewards). ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Robust Post-Training for Generative Recommenders: Why Exponential Reward-Weighted SFT Outperforms RLHF" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>, if rewards are bounded <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{*}(s,a)\in[0,R_{\max}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{*}(s,a)\in[0,R_{\max}]</annotation></semantics></math>, then with probability at least <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\delta</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\pi_{\lambda}}(s)\geq V^{\pi_{\beta}}(s)-R_{\max}\left(e^{2\epsilon/\lambda}-1\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>β</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\pi_{\lambda}}(s)\geq V^{\pi_{\beta}}(s)-R_{\max}\left(e^{2\epsilon/\lambda}-1\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon=\sigma\sqrt{2\log(2|\mathcal{A}|/\delta)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon=\sigma\sqrt{2\log(2|\mathcal{A}|/\delta)}</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\geq 2\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\geq 2\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, this simplifies to:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\pi_{\lambda}}(s)\geq V^{\pi_{\beta}}(s)-\frac{4R_{\max}\epsilon}{\lambda}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>β</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mi>λ</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\pi_{\lambda}}(s)\geq V^{\pi_{\beta}}(s)-\frac{4R_{\max}\epsilon}{\lambda}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4.4 (Temperature-Dependent Bound). ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Robust Post-Training for Generative Recommenders: Why Exponential Reward-Weighted SFT Outperforms RLHF" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a> reveals an explicit robustness-improvement tradeoff controlled by <math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. Large <math id="S4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> suppresses the noise cost (<math id="S4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{2\epsilon/\lambda}-1\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{2\epsilon/\lambda}-1\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>) but drives <math id="S4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\lambda}\to\pi_{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>β</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\lambda}\to\pi_{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding no improvement. Small <math id="S4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> enables aggressive re-ranking but amplifies sensitivity to reward noise. In the regime <math id="S4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\geq 2\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\geq 2\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, the linearized bound <math id="S4.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4R_{\max}\epsilon/\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4R_{\max}\epsilon/\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> shows that the noise cost decays as <math id="S4.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1/\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1/\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>. Given noise level <math id="S4.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> and desired degradation tolerance <math id="S4.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, a sufficient condition for the value degradation to remain within <math id="S4.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is:</p>
<table id="S4.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\geq\frac{2\epsilon}{\log(1+\tau/R_{\max})}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\geq\frac{2\epsilon}{\log(1+\tau/R_{\max})},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p5.2" class="ltx_p">which follows from requiring <math id="S4.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\max}(e^{2\epsilon/\lambda}-1)\leq\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\max}(e^{2\epsilon/\lambda}-1)\leq\tau</annotation></semantics></math>. While this lower bound is conservative due to worst-case analysis over the full action space, it reveals the qualitative structure of the tradeoff: the minimum safe temperature scales linearly with the noise level <math id="S4.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> and inversely with the log-tolerance <math id="S4.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log(1+\tau/R_{\max})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log(1+\tau/R_{\max})</annotation></semantics></math>. In practice, we recommend tuning <math id="S4.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> via validation, as the theoretical lower bound can be significantly loose.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Extension to the MDP Setting</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The objective in Equation (<a href="#S3.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 3.2 Exponential Reward-Weighted Policy Optimization ‣ 3 Background ‣ Robust Post-Training for Generative Recommenders: Why Exponential Reward-Weighted SFT Outperforms RLHF" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) extends directly to the MDP setting:</p>
<table id="S5.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{s\sim d^{\pi_{\beta}},a\sim\pi}\!\left[A^{\pi_{k}}(s,a)\right]-\lambda D_{\mathrm{KL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>β</mi></msub></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{s\sim d^{\pi_{\beta}},a\sim\pi}\!\left[A^{\pi_{k}}(s,a)\right]-\lambda D_{\mathrm{KL}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.E16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{s\sim d^{\pi_{\beta}},a\sim\pi}\!\left[r(s,a)\right]-\underbrace{\mathbb{E}_{s\sim d^{\pi_{\beta}}}\!\left[V^{\pi_{k}}(s)\right]}_{\text{constant w.r.t.}\ \pi}-\lambda D_{\mathrm{KL}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>β</mi></msub></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>β</mi></msub></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mtext>constant w.r.t.</mtext><mo lspace="0.410em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></munder><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{s\sim d^{\pi_{\beta}},a\sim\pi}\!\left[r(s,a)\right]-\underbrace{\mathbb{E}_{s\sim d^{\pi_{\beta}}}\!\left[V^{\pi_{k}}(s)\right]}_{\text{constant w.r.t.}\ \pi}-\lambda D_{\mathrm{KL}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\pi_{\beta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>β</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\pi_{\beta}}</annotation></semantics></math> is fixed and <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\pi_{k}}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\pi_{k}}(s)</annotation></semantics></math> does not depend on <math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>, the value term is constant with respect to <math id="S5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> and drops out. Since <math id="S5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\pi_{\beta}}(s)=\frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=1}^{T}P(s_{t}=s\mid\pi_{\beta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>β</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\pi_{\beta}}(s)=\frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=1}^{T}P(s_{t}=s\mid\pi_{\beta})</annotation></semantics></math> marginalizes over timesteps, the remaining objective satisfies:</p>
<table id="S5.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{s\sim d^{\pi_{\beta}},a\sim\pi}\!\left[r(s,a)\right]=\frac{1}{T}\mathbb{E}_{\tau}\!\left[R(\tau)\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>β</mi></msub></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{s\sim d^{\pi_{\beta}},a\sim\pi}\!\left[r(s,a)\right]=\frac{1}{T}\mathbb{E}_{\tau}\!\left[R(\tau)\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(\tau)=\sum_{t=1}^{T}r(s_{t},a_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(\tau)=\sum_{t=1}^{T}r(s_{t},a_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> is the trajectory-level rewards. Since <math id="S5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is constant with respect to <math id="S5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>, the objective is equivalent to:</p>
<table id="S5.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{\pi}\;\mathbb{E}_{\tau}\!\left[R(\tau)\right]-\lambda D_{\mathrm{KL}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>π</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.447em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{\pi}\;\mathbb{E}_{\tau}\!\left[R(\tau)\right]-\lambda D_{\mathrm{KL}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The contextual bandit setting is recovered as the special case <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=1</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(\tau)=r(s,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(\tau)=r(s,a)</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the objectives are identical, the same Lagrangian derivation applies with <math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> in place of <math id="S5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(s,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(s,a)</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding the trajectory-level optimal policy:</p>
<table id="S5.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}(\tau)\propto\pi_{\beta}(\tau)\exp\!\left(\frac{R(\tau)}{\lambda}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>λ</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}(\tau)\propto\pi_{\beta}(\tau)\exp\!\left(\frac{R(\tau)}{\lambda}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">and the corresponding training objective:</p>
<table id="S5.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E20.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\theta)=-\frac{1}{|\mathcal{D}|}\sum_{\tau\in\mathcal{D}}\exp\!\left(\frac{R(\tau)}{\lambda}\right)\sum_{t=1}^{T}\log\pi_{\theta}(a_{t}\mid s_{t})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></munder><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>λ</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\theta)=-\frac{1}{|\mathcal{D}|}\sum_{\tau\in\mathcal{D}}\exp\!\left(\frac{R(\tau)}{\lambda}\right)\sum_{t=1}^{T}\log\pi_{\theta}(a_{t}\mid s_{t}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The theoretical guarantees of Theorems <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 4.3 (Policy Improvement Under Noisy Rewards). ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Robust Post-Training for Generative Recommenders: Why Exponential Reward-Weighted SFT Outperforms RLHF" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>
and <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4.4 (Temperature-Dependent Bound). ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Robust Post-Training for Generative Recommenders: Why Exponential Reward-Weighted SFT Outperforms RLHF" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a> hold without modification, requiring only that the observed return <math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{R}(\tau)=R^{*}(\tau)+\xi(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{R}(\tau)=R^{*}(\tau)+\xi(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies Assumption <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Assumption 4.2 (Sub-Gaussian Reward Noise). ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Robust Post-Training for Generative Recommenders: Why Exponential Reward-Weighted SFT Outperforms RLHF" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>. This holds under either of the following conditions: (i) if per-step noise terms are independent, sub-Gaussianity of each implies sub-Gaussianity of their sum by standard closure properties; (ii) if per-step noise terms are dependent, as they naturally are along a Markov trajectory, we require only that the trajectory-level aggregate noise <math id="S5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi(\tau)=\sum_{t=1}^{T}\xi_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi(\tau)=\sum_{t=1}^{T}\xi_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is itself sub-Gaussian, which we take as a direct assumption on the noise process. Condition (i) is a special case of (ii).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">One practical consideration is reward variance. Since <math id="S5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> is a sum of up to <math id="S5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> per-step rewards, its variance grows with trajectory length. The theoretically principled way to control the influence of high-variance returns is through <math id="S5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> itself. Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4.4 (Temperature-Dependent Bound). ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ Robust Post-Training for Generative Recommenders: Why Exponential Reward-Weighted SFT Outperforms RLHF" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a> explicitly characterizes this tradeoff, showing the cost of variance reduction in terms of policy improvement. In practice, standardizing <math id="S5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{R}(\tau)=(R(\tau)-\mu)/\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{R}(\tau)=(R(\tau)-\mu)/\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> before exponential weighting serves as a useful preprocessing step that stabilizes the numerical range of weights and makes <math id="S5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> more interpretable across datasets with different return scales, corresponding to a reparametrization <math id="S5.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\to\lambda\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\to\lambda\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> by Observation <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Observation 3.1 (Baseline and Scale Invariance). ‣ 3.2 Exponential Reward-Weighted Policy Optimization ‣ 3 Background ‣ Robust Post-Training for Generative Recommenders: Why Exponential Reward-Weighted SFT Outperforms RLHF" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Experiments</h2>

<figure id="S6.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S6.T1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S6.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Dataset Statistics</span></figcaption>
<table id="S6.T1.5" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S6.T1.5.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S6.T1.5.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt">Dataset</th>
<th id="S6.T1.5.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Users</th>
<th id="S6.T1.5.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Items</th>
